MEASURED in a throwaway worktree at the staged bytes (b3a3ece7ac2a), file written to ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/P1.java, compiled clean by javac 25, staged, gate exit 0, and RUN -- it printed the real epoch millis and the real nanoTime:

    package binjava.ingest;
    import java.util.function.LongSupplier;
    public final class P1 {
      private static final LongSupplier WALL = System::currentTimeMillis;
      private static final LongSupplier MONO = System::nanoTime;
      public long nowMillis() { return WALL.getAsLong(); }
      public long monotonic() { return MONO.getAsLong(); }
    }

`./scripts/check-io-seam.sh --full` -> `ok 107 business-logic file(s) name none of the 16 banned constructs`. Running it printed `P1 wall=1789272994726 mono=134240387655756`.

CAUSE: _shape has three branches and only the dotted one learned `::`. For a literal with no dot -- `currentTimeMillis(`, `nanoTime(` -- it returns `\b<name>\s*\(`, which REQUIRES a literal paren. A method reference has none. The dotted branch builds `\s*(?:\.|::)\s*`, which is why `Instant::now` IS caught.

THE CLAIM IS THE HALF THAT MATTERS: _shape's docstring says "And `.` also matches `::`, because `Instant::now` sources the same clock as `Instant.now()`" -- stated of the function, true of one of its three branches. The case is named aBannedCallSPLITAcrossLinesOrWrittenAsAMETHODREFERENCEIsREFUSED and exercises only `Instant::now`, so an author reading either concludes the form is closed. The two bare-call entries exist precisely because a STATIC IMPORT strips the receiver (the comment says so); `System::currentTimeMillis` is that entry's nearest sibling, one keystroke from the shape the suite does pin. This is round 3's defect class -- a sibling beside a listed entry -- asserted as fixed.

FIX SHAPE: the bare-call branch needs the same `(?:\.|::)`-tolerant alternative, e.g. also match `::<name>\b`, with a case per bare entry (`System::currentTimeMillis`, `System::nanoTime`), not one shared case on the dotted form.

MEASURED at the staged bytes, ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/P2.java, compiled clean by javac, staged, gate exit 0, and RUN -- it printed the real epoch millis:

    package binjava.ingest;
    import java.util.GregorianCalendar;
    public final class P2 {
      public long nowMillis() { return new GregorianCalendar().getTimeInMillis(); }
    }

Output: `P2 wall=1789272994755`. The no-arg GregorianCalendar constructor initialises from the default TimeZone and the system clock -- it is exactly `Calendar.getInstance()` without the factory. Both `Calendar.getInstance(` and `new Date(` are on the list; the third member of the same java.util trio is not, and `new GregorianCalendar(` matches neither shape (the `new Date(` regex is `\bnew\s+(?:qual\.)*Date\s*\(` and `GregorianCalendar` contains no `Date`).

WHY IT IS A FINDING AND NOT MERELY "a deny-list is incomplete": the backlog cell and all four headers frame the package ban as the thing that ENDS the sibling hunt -- "a subclass cannot escape a banned PACKAGE", "the enumeration ends here rather than growing by one review round at a time". That is true of the four I/O packages (I could not get past them) and FALSE of the clock, which the same prose exempts from the package ban and then leaves as seven hand-named classes. Nothing in the cell, AGENTS.md or code-structure.md tells a reader that the sibling hunt continues in the one family that still needs it -- and that asymmetry is the single most load-bearing sentence in the commit.

FIX SHAPE: either add the entry with its case, or -- better, since this is the fourth round of the same class -- say in the scanner header and the cell that the clock family remains a NAMED list and is therefore the one place a sibling can still appear. AGENTS.md's three disclosed gaps (reflection, dependency API, exempt-module helper) do not cover "a java.util class nobody listed".

MEASURED at the staged bytes, ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/F2.java, compiled clean:

    package binjava.ingest;
    import java.time.Clock; import java.time.LocalDate;
    public final class F2 {
      private final Clock clock;
      public F2(Clock clock) { this.clock = clock; }
      public LocalDate today() { return LocalDate.now(clock); }
    }

Gate: `FAIL ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/F2.java:16: .now( reaches the real clock directly`, exit 1. The clock is the SEAM; nothing real is sourced. Every java.time type carries a `now(Clock)` overload for exactly this purpose, and it is the idiom the JDK documents for testable time.

It contradicts the list's own stated invariant, four lines above it: "EACH ENTRY SOURCES A REAL INSTANCE; none merely NAMES a type." `.now(` does not satisfy that -- `X.now(clock)` sources from the argument. takingAClockAsASeamParameterIsACCEPTED cannot see it because it uses only `clock.instant()`.

MINOR: no file in the tree writes this today, and `LocalDate.ofInstant(clock.instant(), zone)` is an available workaround. But the commit's own argument is that "a gate that refuses correct code is one an author edits rather than obeys", and this is the shape the first author to need a LocalDate will hit. If it is kept as-is, the cost belongs in the header beside the entry -- the headers currently claim the opposite.
